<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhön</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhön Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rhön is a range of low volcanic mountains in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> meet. Its highest peak is the Wasserkuppe, at 950 metres, known as the birthplace of gliding. Much of it belonged to the prince-abbots of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a>, and it is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhon.jpg" alt="Rhön" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jörg Braukmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rh%C3%B6n_von_Norden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
